thank you very much for being on the  program now there's been an awful lot of  pitch rolling about what to expect for  from the Autumn statements um by  watering difficult decisions according  to the chancellor do we need to brace  ourselves I think it's going to be quite  tough because what we do know very  clearly is we've got very high inflation  very tight labor market we're going to  be uh have the prospect of pretty low  growth for some time in common with  other economies around the world the IMF  is saying about a third of world  economies will go into recession in the  next year and that's the backdrop with  this huge fiscal hole that needs to be  filled so what we know from that is  they're going to be some very tough  decisions he's going to have to take on  tax and on spending what we don't know  is what exactly they're going to look  like but there is a 50 plus billion  pound fiscal hold to fill and that means  some big decisions I think and we were  going to be paying more tax the chance I  think has said that  um whether it's actually everybody down  to every last member of the public that  pays more tax or not let's wait to see  but I think the point that he's making  there is that the size of that fiscal  hole is such that you're going to  probably have to look at tax measures  that are fairly broadly based if you're  going to bring in the amounts of money  that's required and there's also of  course talk about cuts to public service  and that's going to be alarming for a  lot of people who will be looking at the  state of their public services and  thinking where is the fat to be trimmed  here schools for example you know where  they've got you know talking about  cutting teaching assistants or cutting  back on textbooks at a time when  inflation is already at 11 yes  that's part of what's going to be really  difficult now one of the first decisions  he's going to have to take is what's the  balance between tax Rises and  expenditure cut so we don't know that  yet but the more he leans into tax of  course the less he'll have to lean into  controlling expenditure well you're  absolutely right with inflation as it is  at the moment given that most spending  settlements uh were struck some time ago  when expectations of inflation were much  lower there are going to be those  additional pressures on the public  finances but let's see what he has to  say about that bear in mind of course  the pain here will probably be spread  over several years maybe five years  before we reach the critical point where  his fiscal Target kicks in which I think  will probably be 20 27 28 and it's in  that year that he has to get all these  Sons to add up it doesn't necessarily  have to go for lots of short-term pain  he could spread it you see I this is  where my it's like skepticism kicks in  I'll be honest  uh because obviously there will be some  things that come in earlier  some of these cuts and spending squeezes  a bit of a fantasy designed to reassure  the markets in the short term but in the  long term particularly the ones that as  you say are post the next election it's  not going to happen are they well I  think the critical phrase you've just  used there Sophie is reassure the  markets that's the litmus test so the  markets will have a very good news for  this they will look at the plans that  are put forward and they will say  perhaps I don't think it will happen but  maybe they might say there's too much  pain tomorrow you know it's getting too  easier ride he's pushing it all to the  back beyond the next general election it  doesn't feel credible I don't think  you'll make that mistake so I think  there will be a fairly balanced budget  in terms of what tax and spending are  doing the short and medium term as well  as as well as further down the line and  I want to talk to you a bit about your  department work and pensions at Big  spending departments uh of course now  Jamie hunt told me at the weekend we've  got a big increase in the number of  people who aren't working who aren't  taking part in work even though they  perhaps could is he right yes he's  absolutely right now as it happens  compared to other major economies they  are in a very similar position in fact  we're in no worser position than most  other economies but nonetheless there  are nine million people who are  economically inactive and they fall into  four basic groups one is students then  you've got those that have retired early  you've got those that are long-term uh  sick and you've got those that are  caring have caring responsibilities now  each one of those cohorts has different  levers if you like that you can try and  pull to encourage and support them into  work and what the Prime Minister has  asked me to do is to look at that as a  main project now for the DWP is how do  we really get across all those cohorts  and do whatever we can to make sure we  increase the uptake of those going into  the workplace particularly as that  number's grown a lot during the pandemic  and that's just 600 000 went into  economic inactivity over the period of  time so what levers are you going to  pull then to try and encourage if you  like so these people yeah so the kindest  gloss on it yes so it depends on the  group that you're looking at so for  example if they are already engaged with  the department for work and pensions  they're on benefits one would look at  for example what kind of support are we  providing people to get into work how  frequently frequent is the contact we  have for those for example that are on  Universal Credit uh how many hours do we  expect people to work before we take a  lighter touch approach to that do you  know do we extend the number of hours we  expect people to be in work before we  say right okay we can leave you uh that  support withdraw that support a little  bit so I think there are issues around  conditionality and benefits if you've  retired however early say age 55 and  you're feeling comfortable and you're  not claiming any benefits at all then  you're kind of beyond our boundary but  nonetheless there might be things that  government more generally can do to  encourage those people back into work  flexible working offers for example  making sure that those individuals are  really clear as to what the costs of  their retirement are going to be because  they're working on this assumption that  you know taking it easy at 55 is going  to still work out by the time you reach  65. so so there are all sorts of  different interventions but what I'm  going to do is look at each one of those  cohorts in detail and do the really hard  research kicking the ties to make sure  we come up with the best possible policy  so you're quite open saying that you are  going to be trying to effectively push  some people on benefits back into work  they should be working more in when the  when the Prime Minister appointed me in  the cabinet room he discussed the  various things he wanted me to do on top  of that list was economic uh uh you know  those that are disengage I mean some  will be saying that it sounds you know  could be seen as a bit heartless if you  know a lot of people that particularly  on you know long-term sake who are on or  on caring as you say or on benefits  struggling and trying to make things  work with inflation they'll be looking  at what you're saying and thinking hang  on a minute he's talking about taking my  money away from me and I cannot this is  well well this is well I'm not talking  about taking people's money away and  certainly not for very good reason I  mean if people don't engage and expect  to be supported and not do anything to  look for work then that's a particular  scenario but most people are far from  that particular situation but I think it  is true that there are some within those  various groups that we can help if we  come up with the the right policies to  do that and that's what I think we  should do and we know that of that 9  million 1.7 million are saying that they  want to work  so not everybody there will some  long-term people with long-term  illnesses where it just won't be  appropriate and it won't work and I  accept that but there are millions there  are some Millions for whom work is  actually going to be quite appealing if  we just make it happen  uh now the it it looks from reporting so  the pensions triple lock may be  protected you know the government's  talked a lot about people with the  broader shoulders at bearing at some of  the burden we're expecting to see a  squeeze in pay if you like for public  sector workers  um is it fair that for a retiree many of  whom are wealthy or they're except of  course there is pension poverty that  they might see their pensions go up by  10 along with inflation when public  sector workers could only dream of that  kind of price okay well the first thing  is I I can't confirm or deny whether the  triple lock will stay unfortunately I  have to wait until tomorrow what the  chancellor has to say but in terms of  that 10 of course the up rating that  occurred last time around which was in  April of uh last year uh pegged on the  previous September's inflation rate was  just 3.1 so there's an inflation was  running higher over most of the period  after that so there's an element of  swings and round events but I think the  critical thing about pensioners is that  unlike most of the rest of us there is a  time of life where re-engaging with  Workforce is much more problematic and  difficult and therefore it's much harder  for them to adjust their own economic  situation a public sector  as well if someone could click their  fingers and get a pay rise indeed indeed  so that that's why um the government has  come forward with all these  interventions like for example my  department has been sending eight  million households 60 650 pounds now  they are households typically on UC uh  they're the lowest income households in  the country and then there's 150 pounds  for those with disabilities 300 pounds  for pensioners again  um and uh help with council tax for  those on the lower council tax ban so  there's lots of targeted support that's  going into looking after just the people  that you're referring to okay um I want  to talk to you about Dominic Robb he of  course is facing an investigation into  allegations of bullying and behavior  is it correct that Richie sunac will be  the ultimate Arbiter of that  investigation and all the findings will  be passed to him to make a judgment my  understanding and I think Dominic has  done exactly the right thing there have  been a couple instances of complaints  that have come forward he's gone to the  Prime Minister he's recommended to the  Prime Minister that he looks into this  the pm has agreed when Lord guide is  replaced then there will be somebody to  look into that and I have no doubt that  that individual whoever it is will do so  without fear and favor so it won't be an  Ethics advisor sacked by Boris Johnson  well quite useful to have around the the  prime well the Prime Minister has made  it very clear the the that integrity and  transparency and good governance is  absolutely core to this government's  Mission so I have no doubt whoever  replaces Lord guide conducts this  investigation will do so without fear  favor or indeed interference so I'm  absolutely confident it will be  confident that it will be an independent  uh exercise  um just finally I want to talk a little  bit about the wider culture in  Westminster so you can park the  allegations about Dominic Robb for now  and you know our reporters Liz Bates and  Aggie Schoenberg have been investigating  a bullying in Westminster for a podcast  or Open Secret all parties not just the  conservatives and I just want to read to  you some of the anonymous quotes given  to them  she was always asking me the MP from  Mustafa she was always asking me to do  things that were inappropriate she got  me to scrub stains out the carpet  another one I remember feeling like a  dog in an rspca advert where they hide  behind the sofa when the owners are  coming back and finally I knew she had a  terrible reputation some other MPS  approached me after I left and said oh  my God how did you last that long right  well clearly we don't know the specifics  of all those cases that you've raised  but they sound pretty horrific and  they're unacceptable and that's why I  think the House of Commons as an in  general sort of umbrella to all the MPS  as employers has come a long way  actually with its grievance procedures  and people are encouraged to come  forward now so I think that's the way  forward but it's very sad when you hear  those things I totally agree I can only  speak from my own experience mixing and  mingling with members of parliament okay  I don't spend a lot of time down in the  bars as it happens and perhaps that's  where all the bad behavior goes on but I  found generally speaking that colleagues  are serious people who want to do the  best for their constituency and I say  that right across the house all parties  and generally treat their staff with  great respect but whether exceptions it  needs to be dealt with and I think the  processes are now there for that to  happen Okay thank you very much thank  you

